# ffi/logs/rust/stage3-reproducibility.log
# W3: is a ratio reproducible across builds? The M1 and M2 tables re-taken under R4 as
# sharpened, and a verdict on which column is right. 2026-09-18
#
# CONFIGURATION (R7)
#   machine        4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.80GHz, 15 GB, Ubuntu 24.04.4, Linux 6.18.44 x86_64,
#                  container, NOT an isolated host. No CPU pinning, no governor control.
#   rust           rustc/cargo 1.94.1, profile release (opt-level 3, lto off, PIE)
#   incumbent      prost 0.14.4, bytes 1.12.1 -- UNCHANGED in the lockfile at every commit
#                  checked (cc7f68c6, d03c5161, 7fb30be5, HEAD), so no dependency bump is
#                  in play
#   LINKAGE        cdylib (libak_core.so), dynamic linker
#   guard          on;  content ASCII;  bench settings 15 rounds, 25 ms per round
#   arms rebuilt   EVERY arm from one `cargo build` per group, so no row comes from a
#                  different binary than the row beside it
#
# ============================================================================
# THE VERDICT, FIRST
# ============================================================================
#   1. **A ratio IS reproducible on this container, to about +-0.02 to +-0.05.** Three
#      builds, five to six runs, every arm rebuilt together: most rows have a total spread
#      under 0.05, and the ACROSS-BUILD component is no larger than the SAME-BINARY
#      component on nearly every row. Layout is not the driver.
#   2. **The published encode figures are 0.10 to 0.27 away from today** -- five to ten
#      times that band. That is not noise and it is not a rebuild artefact.
#   3. **AND THE PUBLISHED COLUMN CANNOT BE RE-DERIVED AT ALL.** `cc7f68c6`, the commit that
#      produced `stage2-four-arms-M1.log`, does not contain the benchmark binary: the bins
#      were untracked until `7fb30be5` (defect D19, the root `.gitignore`'s `[Bb]in/`). A
#      worktree at `cc7f68c6` fails to build with "can't find bin `bench`". **The oldest
#      commit from which the M1 table can be rebuilt is `7fb30be5`, and it agrees with today
#      (core-ffi-rust P1.2 encode 0.992) and not with the published table (0.706-0.716).**
#   4. So the question "which column is right" is **not answerable by re-measurement**, and
#      the answer is not "the container is unstable" -- it is stable. The published column
#      is unreproducible because the code that produced it was never committed. **The table
#      below is the one that can be checked, and it is the one to use.**
#   5. **What that costs the headline.** `core-ffi-rust` on P1.2 encode reads 0.982 today
#      against 0.706-0.716 published: through the C ABI the core is **at parity with prost
#      on encode for the uniform payloads, not thirty percent faster**. What survives intact
#      is the no-boundary arm: `core-native` is 0.42 to 0.54 of prost on every encode row,
#      so the codec is about twice prost's speed and the C ABI gives that back.
#
# ============================================================================
# HOW REPRODUCIBILITY WAS MEASURED, SINCE "REBUILD" ALONE VARIES NOTHING
# ============================================================================
#   The release binary is BIT-IDENTICAL across rebuilds of unchanged source (checked:
#   sha256 90d08e36d17173be twice, after `touch`ing lib.rs). So rebuilding proves nothing.
#   What changed between the published table and today is that the crate GREW, which moves
#   code and data addresses. `gen/stability.sh` therefore perturbs the LAYOUT and nothing
#   else: k exported no-op functions in the harness that no arm calls, k in {0, 3, 11}. The
#   binary hash changes every time (4d61ea4e -> 762c2fde -> 8cbb9b0c) and the size barely
#   moves (1180096 -> 1180096 -> 1180104), which is what a pure layout perturbation looks
#   like. Two runs per build; the sweep was stopped after three builds because the answer
#   was already outside any plausible noise band.
#
# ============================================================================
# MEASUREMENT HAZARDS (R9)
# ============================================================================
#   1. Two rows are genuinely noisy and their figures should be read as bands, not values:
#      P1.3 decode (total spread 0.12 to 0.24 across arms) and P2.1 encode `core-ffi-rust`
#      (0.16). P1.3 has 300 all-absent elements and P2.1 has ONE element, so both are
#      dominated by per-message cost.
#   2. Three builds and six runs is a small sample for a distribution. The columns below are
#      min / median / max, not a fitted interval.
#   3. This says nothing about why the published numbers differ, only that they cannot be
#      reproduced or rebuilt. A different physical host is the obvious candidate and is not
#      checkable from inside the container.
#   4. Everything here is ASCII, guard on, one toolchain.
#
# ============================================================================
